00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,540
Voiced consonants and reduced vowels The strong intonation in American English

2
00:00:05,540 --> 00:00:10,540
creates certain tendencies in your spoken language. Here are four consistent

3
00:00:10,540 --> 00:00:15,620
conditions that are a result of intonation's tense peaks and relaxed

4
00:00:15,620 --> 00:00:23,200
valleys. 1. Reduced Vowels You were introduced to reduced vowels in Chapter 1.

5
00:00:23,560 --> 00:00:28,380
They appear in the valleys that are formed by the strong peaks of intonation. The

6
00:00:28,380 --> 00:00:32,980
more you reduce the words in the valleys, the smoother and more natural your speech

7
00:00:32,980 --> 00:00:37,760
will sound. A characteristic of reduced vowels is that your throat muscles should

8
00:00:37,760 --> 00:00:43,160
be very relaxed. This will allow the unstressed vowels to reduce toward the

9
00:00:43,160 --> 00:00:48,640
schwa. Neutral vowels take less energy and muscularity to produce than tense vowels.

10
00:00:49,240 --> 00:00:57,820
For example, the word unbelievable should only have one hard vowel, unbelievable. 2.

11
00:00:58,240 --> 00:01:04,640
Voiced consonants The mouth muscles are relaxed to create a voiced sound like Z or

12
00:01:04,640 --> 00:01:13,060
D. For unvoiced consonants such as S or T, they are sharp and tense. Relaxing your

13
00:01:13,060 --> 00:01:19,360
muscles will simultaneously reduce your vowels and voice your consonants. Think of

14
00:01:19,360 --> 00:01:25,960
voiced consonants as reduced consonants. Both reduced consonants and reduced vowels

15
00:01:26,040 --> 00:01:31,600
are unconsciously preferred by a native speaker of American English. This explains

16
00:01:31,600 --> 00:01:41,240
why T so frequently becomes D and S becomes Z. Get it, Izda. 3. Like sound

17
00:01:41,240 --> 00:01:47,420
with like sound. It's not easy to change horses midstream, so when you have a

18
00:01:47,420 --> 00:01:53,760
voiced consonant, let the consonant that follows it be voiced as well. In the verb

19
00:01:53,760 --> 00:02:02,080
used, for example, the S is really a Z, so it's followed by D. The phrase used to, on

20
00:02:02,080 --> 00:02:08,200
the other hand, has a real S, so it's followed by T. Vowels are, by definition,

21
00:02:08,540 --> 00:02:14,260
voiced. So when one is followed by a common, reducible word, it'll change that

22
00:02:14,260 --> 00:02:21,760
word's first sound, like the preposition to, which will change to duh. The only way

23
00:02:21,760 --> 00:02:27,680
to get it is to practice all the time. Again, this will take time. In the

24
00:02:27,680 --> 00:02:31,840
beginning, work on recognizing these patterns when you hear them. When you're

25
00:02:31,840 --> 00:02:35,520
confident that you understand the structure beneath these sounds and you can

26
00:02:35,520 --> 00:02:40,900
intuit where they belong, you can start to try them out. It's not advisable to

27
00:02:40,900 --> 00:02:46,020
memorize one reduced word and stick it into an otherwise overpronounced sentence.

28
00:02:46,320 --> 00:02:53,920
It would sound strange. 4. Relax. You've probably noticed that the preceding three

29
00:02:53,920 --> 00:02:58,380
conditions, as well as other areas that we've covered, such as liaisons and the

30
00:02:58,380 --> 00:03:05,440
schwa, have one thing in common. The idea that it's physically easier this way. This

31
00:03:05,440 --> 00:03:09,180
is one of the most remarkable characteristics of American English. You

32
00:03:09,180 --> 00:03:13,600
need to relax your mouth and throat muscles, except for ah, ah, and other

33
00:03:13,600 --> 00:03:19,360
tense vowels. And let the sounds flow smoothly out. If you find yourself tensing

34
00:03:19,360 --> 00:03:25,120
up, pursing your lips or tightening your throat, you're gonna strangle and lose the

35
00:03:25,120 --> 00:03:29,580
sound you're pursuing. Relax, relax, relax.
